"For each item below, fetch its source and check that the recorded quote appears on the page and supports the item. A card item is a field of the plan in the producer's words, with the sources it cites: check each factual claim in it against those sources, and any value it carries against the page; what the plan will build, measure or predict needs no source, but a factual claim with no source is UNSUPPORTED. For a cost input, check that the page states the number for the setting given; whether it applies to this plan is not yours to judge. When the page states a different number for a num or rate item, give it as page_value. Decide only from the pages you fetch and the text below; do not read files. Return one status per item, under its ref: SUPPORTED, PARTIAL, UNSUPPORTED, CONTRADICTED or UNREACHABLE, with the page sentence you relied on.
Items, one per line:
{\"ref\":\"card:question\",\"text\":\"Does feeding many GPUs to the block-tridiagonal Newton solve shorten its depth from T ordered steps to log2(T)?\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:hypothesis\",\"text\":\"Parallel cyclic reduction solves the tridiagonal Newton correction in log2(T) rounds instead of T, so GPU width shortens the sequential part [s3].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s3\",\"url\":\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cusolverdx/get_started/functions/gtsv.html\",\"quote\":\"The Thomas algorithm, based on LU decomposition, is efficient to solve tridiagonal systems but inherently sequential, while the PCR algorithm is more computationally expensive but parallelizable. The hybrid combination of the two algorithms is used to achieve the best performance for different problem sizes.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:sequential_part\",\"text\":\"The block-bidiagonal Newton correction is a forward substitution: each row waits on the previous, T ordered steps.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:method\",\"text\":\"Cyclic reduction halves the system each round and solves independent rows together, so log2(T) rounds replace T ordered steps [s3].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s3\",\"url\":\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cusolverdx/get_started/functions/gtsv.html\",\"quote\":\"The Thomas algorithm, based on LU decomposition, is efficient to solve tridiagonal systems but inherently sequential, while the PCR algorithm is more computationally expensive but parallelizable. The hybrid combination of the two algorithms is used to achieve the best performance for different problem sizes.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:if_lever\",\"text\":\"As equation count N grows, cyclic-reduction wall-clock grows like log2(N) and beats the sequential Thomas solve by a widening margin.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:if_not\",\"text\":\"At feasible N the parallel solver never beats sequential: extra work and memory traffic dominate, and one GPU has too few threads to hide them.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:test_summary\",\"text\":\"Time a sequential Thomas tridiagonal solve against parallel cyclic reduction on one A100, sweeping N=2^10 to 2^24, checking both match NVIDIA's gtsv reference and batched independent solves.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:closest_prior\",\"text\":\"Zhang, Cohen and Owens benchmarked cyclic reduction, PCR and recursive doubling on a GTX 280, finding PCR does fewer steps, more work, and 28x over sequential LAPACK [s2].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s2\",\"url\":\"https://research.nvidia.com/sites/default/files/pubs/2010-01_Fast-Tridiagonal-Solvers/Zhang_Fast_2009.pdf\",\"quote\":\"We find that CR enjoys linear algorithm complexity but suffers from more algorithmic steps and bank conflicts, while PCR and RD have fewer algorithmic steps but do more work each step.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:left_open\",\"text\":\"No published result times PCR against an on-GPU sequential Thomas on a modern A100 across N up to 2^24, so the crossover is unmeasured.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:anchor\",\"text\":\"Written PCR and Thomas kernels must match NVIDIA's gtsv tridiagonal solve and a CPU dense solve to 1e-10 relative residual.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:limits\",\"text\":\"Shows one tridiagonal solve's wall-clock scaling on one modern GPU, not the end-to-end parallel-in-time method, whose iteration count the dynamics sets.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"src:s3\",\"url\":\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cusolverdx/get_started/functions/gtsv.html\",\"quote\":\"The Thomas algorithm, based on LU decomposition, is efficient to solve tridiagonal systems but inherently sequential, while the PCR algorithm is more computationally expensive but parallelizable. The hybrid combination of the two algorithms is used to achieve the best performance for different problem sizes.\",\"supports\":\"Thomas is sequential and PCR is parallel (the depth claim); NVIDIA ships a PCR+Thomas hybrid, used as the independent reference.\"}
{\"ref\":\"src:s2\",\"url\":\"https://research.nvidia.com/sites/default/files/pubs/2010-01_Fast-Tridiagonal-Solvers/Zhang_Fast_2009.pdf\",\"quote\":\"We find that CR enjoys linear algorithm complexity but suffers from more algorithmic steps and bank conflicts, while PCR and RD have fewer algorithmic steps but do more work each step.\",\"supports\":\"PCR has fewer (logarithmic) steps but more work per step; the prior GPU benchmark and the 28x speedup proxy.\"}
{\"ref\":\"num:speedup_at_nmax\",\"name\":\"PCR/Thomas speedup at n_max\",\"value\":28,\"unit\":\"x\",\"basis\":\"PROXY\",\"quote\":\"Our GPU solvers achieve up to a 28x speedup over a sequential LAPACK solver, and a 12x speedup over a multi-threaded CPU solver.\",\"page_setting\":\"2009 NVIDIA GTX 280 vs sequential LAPACK dgtsv (CPU), at their largest N\",\"differs_in\":[\"2009 GTX 280 (30 SMs) vs 2020 A100 (108 SMs, 1555 GB/s)\",\"baseline CPU LAPACK dgtsv vs my on-GPU Thomas kernel\",\"their N ~ hundreds vs my N up to 2^24\"],\"url\":\"https://research.nvidia.com/sites/default/files/pubs/2010-01_Fast-Tridiagonal-Solvers/Zhang_Fast_2009.pdf\"}
